@* FR-119 and FR-121: what a driver fills in at the door.

   Dialog content rather than a route. The parcel is handed over while the driver is standing on the
   list of their own deliveries, so the capture belongs over that row - a route would lose which row
   it was and a back button would leave a half-typed capture behind.

   The order below is the design's, and it is the order the act itself has: which parcel this is, who
   took it, their signature, the photographs, and the one action that sends them. The summary leads
   because it is the only thing on the panel a driver checks rather than fills in - a capture filed
   against the wrong row is evidence about the wrong delivery, and the dialog's own title says only
   that a hand-over is being recorded.

   The coordinates are read from the device and are neither typed nor editable, which is FR-119 read
   literally: the point recorded is where the capture happened. A browser that refuses geolocation
   therefore cannot capture, and the panel says so - inventing a fallback, the dropoff point say,
   would record a place the hand-over did not happen. The design draws none of this block, and it
   stays all the same: without it every capture is refused 422 naming a field this panel never
   offered to fill in, after five photographs have already been streamed up the circuit.

   Failures render through FailureKeys.For, exactly as the dispatch board does, so the dialog and the
   REST envelope resolve the same refusal through the same catalogue (NFR-3).

   The @@if blocks are independent rather than if/else chains and every handler is built in code: an
   `else` reads to NFR-14's source scan as a rendered English word, and a `=>` inside an attribute
   closes the tag as far as that scan is concerned. *@
@* The picker ids are built here, and an id is a token rather than a formatted quantity - so the
   figure in it is written under the invariant culture like every other identifier in this product. *@
@using System.Globalization
@inject IProofOfDeliveryService Proofs
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer
@inherits CircuitScopedComponent
@implements IAsyncDisposable

@* CaptureLocation is the matrix row this keeps: the panel draws no coordinate box, so a refusal
   naming it has no input to sit under and would vanish if the banner hid it (FR-83). *@
<DtFailureBanner Failures="_failures" InlineFields="@InlineFields" />

<div class="dt-proof-form">
    @* Which parcel is being signed for. The call site already holds the whole row - it is what the
       driver pressed Record on - so this costs a parameter rather than a read, and a panel that
       named nothing was a panel a driver could only trust by remembering which button they pressed.

       The dropoff rather than the pickup: this is the door being stood at. Address when one has
       resolved and coordinates when none has (FR-94), which is the same reading both delivery
       screens give the same value. *@
    @if (Summary is { } delivery)
    {
        <DtCard Class="dt-proof-summary">
            <div class="dt-proof-summary-body">
                <p class="dt-proof-summary-place dt-type-h3">
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden">@Localizer["Dropoff"]</span>
                    <Icon Name="IconName.Location" />@Describe(delivery.Dropoff)
                </p>

                @* The parcel as one line, the way the own-deliveries row states it: what is being moved,
                   how heavy it is, and where it has got to. The weight carries its unit, because a bare
                   figure beside a description is a number with no name. *@
                <p class="dt-proof-summary-meta dt-type-small">
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden">@Localizer["Package"]</span>
                    <span>@delivery.PackageDetails</span>
                    <span>@delivery.PackageWeightKg @Localizer["WeightUnit"]</span>
                    <DeliveryStatusLabel Status="delivery.Status" />
                </p>

                <p class="dt-proof-summary-meta dt-type-small">
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden">@Localizer["DeliveryWindow"]</span>
                    <span>@Window(delivery)</span>
                </p>
            </div>
        </DtCard>
    }

    <div class="dt-proof-field">
        <label class="form-label" for="@_recipientFieldId">@Localizer["ProofRecipientName"]</label>
        <input id="@_recipientFieldId" class="form-control" type="text"
               maxlength="@CaptureProofCommandValidator.RecipientNameMaximumLength"
               @bind="_recipientName" />
        <DtFieldError Field="RecipientName" Failures="_failures" />
    </div>

    <div class="dt-proof-field">
        <span class="form-label">@Localizer["ProofSignature"]</span>
        <DtSignaturePad @ref="_signature" />
        <DtFieldError Field="Signature" Failures="_failures" />
    </div>

    <div class="dt-proof-field">
        @* The label and the count on one line, which is the design's badge: a driver at a door
           checks how many they have taken against how many they are allowed far more often than
           they read the sentence explaining it. The figures are numerals rather than a formatted
           sentence, and the word in front of them is hidden - a screen reader hears "photographs
           chosen, 2 of 5" and the eye reads the pill. *@
        <div class="dt-proof-legend">
            <span class="form-label">@Localizer["ProofPhotos"]</span>
            <DtBadge Class="dt-proof-photo-count">
                <span class="dt-visually-hidden">@Localizer["ProofPhotoCount"]</span>
                @if (OverTheLimit)
                {
                    <span>@Localizer["ProofPhotosOverLimit"]</span>
                }
                @if (!OverTheLimit)
                {
                    <span>@_photos.Count / @ProofAssetRules.MaximumPhotos</span>
                }
            </DtBadge>
        </div>

        @* A tile for each chosen file and the Add tile after them, which is the whole of the photo
           control: the <input> itself is inside the Add tile, filling it, so the tile IS the
           picker rather than a button wired to one. It is transparent rather than display:none,
           because a hidden input is an input a keyboard cannot reach, and the tile shows the focus
           it takes.

           The tile shows the file's name and a glyph rather than the photograph. A thumbnail would
           mean reading up to five 5 MiB files into base64 and pushing them back down the circuit to
           be looked at, which is the cost of the upload paid twice before the driver has pressed
           anything.

           `role="list"` because `list-style: none` takes the list semantics away in Safari and
           VoiceOver, and a strip of photographs is exactly the thing whose count a reader wants
           announced.

           Adding a photograph adds one: the chosen files accumulate and each tile carries its own
           Remove, so a driver who picked the wrong picture takes it out rather than starting the
           whole capture again. Nothing here refuses a file - the panel is not a second, stricter
           definition of a valid proof (AD-9) - so a selection over the limit is still sent and
           still refused by CaptureProofCommandValidator with the rule that names the ceiling,
           which is the same answer the REST surface gives. *@
        <ul class="dt-proof-photos" role="list">
            @foreach (var photo in _photos)
            {
                <li class="dt-proof-photo">
                    <Icon Name="IconName.Camera" />
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden">@Localizer["ProofPhotoTile"]</span>
                    <span class="dt-proof-photo-name">@photo.Name</span>
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm dt-proof-photo-remove"
                            aria-label="@Localizer["ProofPhotoRemove"]" @onclick="RemoveHandler(photo)">
                        <Icon Name="IconName.Close" />
                    </button>
                </li>
            }

            @* The Add tile is always rendered and hidden at the limit rather than dropped, and that
               is load-bearing rather than tidy. Blazor's file control empties its own file table on
               every change event and clears the input's value on every click, so a file picked on an
               input that has since fired again cannot be read at all - "There is no file with ID N".
               Accumulating therefore means one input per gesture, and those inputs have to stay in
               the page until the capture is sent. `display: none` hides the tile without taking them
               out of the document, which removing the <li> would.

               Only the newest input is live. The rest are disabled, so they are neither clickable
               nor reachable by a keyboard and exist only to keep the files they were given readable.

               The accept list is the same allowlist ProofAssetRules holds, stated as the browser
               spells it. It narrows the picker; it refuses nothing. `image/*` would be a widening,
               not a simplification: it would let an iPhone's HEIC through to be refused afterwards.

               There is no `capture` attribute, and its absence is the decision: a present `capture`
               makes a phone open the camera and hand back a single frame, overriding `multiple` on
               exactly the device this panel is drawn for. Without it the phone still offers the
               camera in its own chooser, and one gesture can still pick five out of the gallery. *@
            <li class="dt-proof-photo dt-proof-photo-add @AddTileClass">
                <label class="dt-proof-photo-label" for="@PickerId(_gestures)">
                    <Icon Name="IconName.Camera" />@Localizer["ProofPhotoAdd"]
                </label>
                @foreach (var gesture in Enumerable.Range(0, _gestures + 1))
                {
                    <InputFile id="@PickerId(gesture)" class="dt-proof-picker" multiple
                               disabled="@Spent(gesture)"
                               accept="image/png,image/jpeg,image/webp" OnChange="Choose" />
                }
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="dt-proof-hint">@Localizer["ProofPhotosHint"]</p>
        <DtFieldError Field="Assets" Failures="_failures" />
    </div>

    <div class="dt-proof-field">
        <span class="form-label">@Localizer["ProofLocation"]</span>
        @if (_location is null && _locationRefused)
        {
            <p class="dt-proof-hint" role="status">@Localizer["ProofLocationRefused"]</p>
        }
        @if (_location is null && !_locationRefused)
        {
            <p class="dt-proof-hint" role="status">@Localizer["ProofLocationPending"]</p>
        }
        @if (_location is { } point)
        {
            <DtMap Value="point" Picker="false" />
        }
    </div>

    @* Pinned to the bottom of the dialog's own scroll box, full width, inside thumb reach: a driver
       holding a parcel one-handed should not have to scroll a form to find the button that files
       it. Sticky rather than a dialog action, because the panel is dialog CONTENT - the foot
       belongs to DtDialog, and putting the capture there would move it out of the component two
       tests already read as one piece.

       Disabled while there is no capture point as well as while a capture is in flight. FR-119
       records where the hand-over happened and the form draws no coordinate field, so a submit
       without one can only ever return a 422 naming CaptureLocation - a refusal about something
       this panel never offered to fill in, paid for with every photograph streamed up the circuit
       first. The sentence above the map is what the driver acts on instead.

       The design says the action stays enabled and shows the misses afterwards. That reading holds
       for the boxes on this panel and not for this one: every other refusal has an input to land
       under, and this one has none. *@
    <div class="dt-proof-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary dt-proof-submit" disabled="@(_saving || _location is null)"
                @onclick="CaptureAsync">
            @if (_saving)
            {
                <Icon Name="IconName.Proof" />@Localizer["ProofSaving"]
            }
            @if (!_saving)
            {
                <Icon Name="IconName.Proof" />@Localizer["ProofCaptureAction"]
            }
        </button>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The boxes this screen renders a <c>DtFieldError</c> for. Stated once and bound by every
    /// banner on the page: the list repeated per banner is exactly the drift FR-83 is broken by,
    /// and a screen carries the banner more than once because a dialog opened with showModal()
    /// leaves the page behind it inert.
    /// </summary>
    private const string InlineFields = "RecipientName,Signature,Assets";

    private const string ModulePath = "./Components/Pages/ProofCapture.razor.js";

    /// <summary>
    /// Generated rather than fixed: two of these on one screen must not share an id, or a label
    /// would point at the wrong field.
    /// </summary>
    private readonly string _recipientFieldId = $"dt-proof-recipient-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    /// <summary>
    /// The prefix every picker's id is built from. Generated rather than fixed for the reason
    /// <see cref="_recipientFieldId"/> is, and a prefix rather than an id because this control is
    /// one input per gesture: see the Add tile's comment for why.
    /// </summary>
    private readonly string _photosFieldId = $"dt-proof-photos-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    /// <summary>
    /// How many times the driver has picked. The newest picker is number <c>_gestures</c> and is the
    /// live one; every lower number has already fired and is kept only so the files it handed over
    /// stay readable.
    /// </summary>
    private int _gestures;

    private IReadOnlyList<IBrowserFile> _photos = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<ScreenFailure> _failures = [];
    private DtSignaturePad? _signature;
    private IJSObjectReference? _module;
    private MapLocation? _location;
    private string? _recipientName;
    private bool _locationRefused;
    private bool _saving;

    /// <summary>The delivery this capture is about.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public int DeliveryId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The row the capture is being filed against, so the panel can say which parcel is being signed
    /// for.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A parameter rather than a read. The own-deliveries screen holds the whole
    /// <see cref="AssignedDeliverySummary"/> already - it is the row whose Record button opened this
    /// dialog - so asking a capability for it again would be a round trip for data the caller is
    /// standing on.
    /// <para>
    /// Optional, and deliberately not <c>EditorRequired</c>: nothing on the capture path reads it -
    /// the command carries <see cref="DeliveryId"/> - so a panel without it still captures correctly
    /// and simply says less. The card is then withheld rather than drawn empty, because a summary
    /// naming nothing is worse than no summary: it reads as a delivery whose details failed to load.
    /// The one call site passes the row it is already holding.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public AssignedDeliverySummary? Summary { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised once a proof has committed, so the screen behind the dialog can withdraw the capture
    /// action and refetch. A proof is captured once (FR-123), so this fires at most once per dialog.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnCaptured { get; set; }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
        {
            return;
        }

        // In OnAfterRenderAsync rather than OnInitializedAsync, so static rendering never reaches
        // IJSRuntime - and because there is no position to ask for until there is a browser.
        _module = await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", ModulePath);

        var point = await _module.InvokeAsync<double[]?>("currentPosition");

        // A refusal, a timeout and a browser with no geolocation at all are one answer here: there
        // are no coordinates, so there is no capture. The panel says which half is missing rather
        // than failing at submit with a validation message about a field nobody can see.
        if (point is [var latitude, var longitude])
        {
            _location = new MapLocation(latitude, longitude);
        }
        else
        {
            _locationRefused = true;
        }

        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        // Called by hand, and first. The renderer disposes a component through IAsyncDisposable when
        // it has one and never also through IDisposable, so the base's Dispose - the one thing that
        // cancels this panel's circuit token - is unreachable on this screen unless it is invoked
        // from here. The base cancels and deliberately does not dispose the source, so the module
        // release below and anything still in flight both keep a readable, cancelled token.
        Dispose();

        if (_module is null)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            await _module.DisposeAsync();
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // The circuit went away before the component did; there is no module left to release.
        }
    }

    private void Choose(InputFileChangeEventArgs change)
    {
        // The cap is `change.FileCount` rather than a figure of our own, so this call can never be
        // the thing that refuses. GetMultipleFiles throws InvalidOperationException the moment more
        // files were picked than the cap allows, and a throw inside an event handler is not a
        // refusal - it is an unhandled exception on the circuit, which takes the whole screen down
        // instead of showing a message.
        //
        // Every file therefore reaches the command, and CaptureProofCommandValidator refuses a
        // selection over the limit with the rule that names it (FR-119). That is the same answer the
        // REST surface gives, which is what AD-9 is for: this panel is not a second, stricter
        // definition of a valid proof, and it is not a kinder one either.
        //
        // Appended rather than assigned: the tile grid and an Add action promise that a second pick
        // adds to the first, and replacing meant a driver who took four photographs and then added
        // one was left holding one. The gesture is counted here, which retires the input that just
        // fired and puts a fresh one behind the Add tile - see the Add tile's comment for why the
        // spent ones have to stay in the page.
        _photos = [.. _photos, .. change.GetMultipleFiles(change.FileCount)];
        _gestures++;
        _failures = [];
    }

    /// <summary>A tile's Remove, bound as a delegate so no lambda has to live in markup.</summary>
    private Action RemoveHandler(IBrowserFile photo) => () => Remove(photo);

    /// <summary>
    /// Takes one photograph out of the selection.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The other half of appending, and the reason a wrong pick is no longer a dead end: without it
    /// a driver who chose five unusable photographs could only escape by closing the dialog, which
    /// loses the recipient's name and their signature with it.
    /// <para>
    /// By reference rather than by name or index, because a gallery happily yields two files called
    /// the same thing and the list is rebuilt on every pick. The input the file came from is left
    /// exactly where it is: it costs nothing, and it is still the owner of any sibling picked in the
    /// same gesture.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="photo">The file whose tile was dismissed.</param>
    private void Remove(IBrowserFile photo)
    {
        _photos = [.. _photos.Where(chosen => !ReferenceEquals(chosen, photo))];

        // The refusal on the page was about the selection that has just changed, so it is no longer
        // about anything - the same reason picking clears it.
        _failures = [];
    }

    /// <summary>The id of one picker. One per gesture, all sharing this panel's generated prefix.</summary>
    /// <param name="gesture">Which pick the input belongs to.</param>
    private string PickerId(int gesture) =>
        _photosFieldId + "-" + gesture.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a picker has already been used. A spent input is disabled rather than removed: it is
    /// the only thing keeping the files it handed over readable, and disabling it takes it out of
    /// the keyboard's path and the pointer's without taking it out of the document.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="gesture">Which pick the input belongs to.</param>
    private bool Spent(int gesture) => gesture != _gestures;

    /// <summary>
    /// The modifier the Add tile wears once five photographs have been chosen: the design withdraws
    /// it there. Hidden rather than dropped, because the pickers inside it are still holding files.
    /// </summary>
    private string AddTileClass =>
        OffersAnotherPhoto(_photos.Count) ? string.Empty : "dt-proof-photo-add--full";

    /// <summary>Whether more has been chosen than a proof may carry.</summary>
    private bool OverTheLimit => IsOverTheLimit(_photos.Count);

    private async Task CaptureAsync()
    {
        _saving = true;

        // Opened here and disposed in the finally below, whatever happened: a browser file stream is
        // a read over the circuit, and one left open is a read the connection is still holding.
        var opened = new List<Stream>();

        try
        {
            var assets = new List<ProofAssetUpload>();

            if (await SignatureAsync() is { } signature)
            {
                opened.Add(signature.Content);
                assets.Add(signature);
            }

            foreach (var photo in _photos)
            {
                var upload = Photo(photo);

                opened.Add(upload.Content);
                assets.Add(upload);
            }

            // Nothing is judged here. An absent signature, no photographs, too many, a disallowed
            // type and an oversized file are all refused by CaptureProofCommandValidator, which runs
            // whether the capture came from this panel or from the REST surface - so this screen
            // cannot be the second, kinder definition of a valid proof (AD-9).
            await Proofs.CaptureAsync(
                DeliveryId,
                new CaptureProofCommand(
                    _recipientName,
                    new LocationInput(_location?.Latitude, _location?.Longitude),
                    assets),
                CircuitToken);

            _failures = [];
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            // Reported rather than thrown: the dialog is open over a screen that is still usable,
            // and taking the whole circuit down for one panel would lose it.
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            return;
        }
        catch (Exception failure) when (Reports(failure))
        {
            // The arm no other screen in this product needs, and the reason is AD-26: this is the
            // only action whose capability calls a network port inline - it mints a key before its
            // transaction and writes the bytes after the commit, and either can fail. An unreachable
            // object store raises the SDK's own exception and an unconfigured one raises
            // InvalidOperationException, and neither is a DriveTrackException - so without this they
            // would escape the panel and kill the circuit, which loses the screen behind it as well.
            //
            // The filter is what makes an arm this wide safe to keep, and it is the base's rule
            // rather than this file's: a driver who closes the dialog mid-capture cancels the
            // circuit token, and without it this catch would intercept the resulting
            // OperationCanceledException before ComponentBase could discard it - reporting a
            // torn-down panel's quiet unwind to nobody as COMMON_UNEXPECTED_ERROR. Letting that
            // one through leaves this screen on the same path as every other one, while an
            // outbound port's own cancellation, raised while the panel is still open, is still
            // reported below.
            //
            // Reported as COMMON_UNEXPECTED_ERROR, which is what the REST surface answers for the
            // same failure (AD-7). What it leaves behind depends on where the store failed, and the
            // panel cannot tell: a refusal while minting leaves nothing at all, and a refusal while
            // writing leaves a committed proof whose assets are missing. So the message says only
            // that the capture failed, and this arm returns - the dialog stays open with the failure
            // on it rather than closing as a success would, and the screen behind is left as it was.
            //
            // A retry is the driver's next move only in the first case. In the second the proof
            // exists, so a second capture is refused as DELIVERY_PROOF_ALREADY_CAPTURED (FR-123) and
            // the evidence has to be sorted out by someone who can see both the row and the bucket.
            // Telling the two apart from here would need the capability to say which side of its
            // commit it failed on, which it does not.
            _failures = [new ScreenFailure(null, nameof(ErrorCode.COMMON_UNEXPECTED_ERROR))];

            return;
        }
        finally
        {
            foreach (var stream in opened)
            {
                await stream.DisposeAsync();
            }

            _saving = false;
        }

        // Outside the try. The proof has committed, so a failure refreshing the screen afterwards is
        // a failed read, and reporting it as a refused capture would tell a driver their evidence
        // was lost when it was not.
        await OnCaptured.InvokeAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The drawing as an upload, or null when the pad is untouched.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The base64 the pad answers is decoded once, here, so the capability is handed a stream like
    /// any other asset and nothing below this component knows an encoding was ever involved. A
    /// <c>MemoryStream</c> rather than a browser file stream because the bytes came from a canvas
    /// and were never a file.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task<ProofAssetUpload?> SignatureAsync()
    {
        if (_signature is null)
        {
            return null;
        }

        if (await _signature.ReadPngBase64Async() is not { Length: > 0 } payload)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var bytes = Convert.FromBase64String(payload);

        return new ProofAssetUpload(
            ProofAssetKind.Signature,
            "image/png",
            bytes.LongLength,
            new MemoryStream(bytes, writable: false));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A chosen file as an upload.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A file above the cap is carried as an empty stream with its real declared length, so the
    /// validator refuses it by length and not one byte of it is ever read. Opening it first would
    /// throw <see cref="IOException"/> out of the browser file API before the validator had a chance
    /// to say anything at all, which is a 500 for a file the contract has a 422 for.
    /// <para>
    /// Internal and static so it can be asserted directly, for the reason
    /// <c>MyDeliveries.OffersProofCapture</c> is: static rendering never reaches
    /// <c>OnAfterRenderAsync</c> and dispatches no events, so nothing in the suite runs this panel —
    /// and dropping the ternary would leave every test green while a driver who picked one large
    /// photograph met "try again" instead of "this image is too big".
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="file">The file the driver picked.</param>
    internal static ProofAssetUpload Photo(IBrowserFile file) =>
        new(
            ProofAssetKind.Photo,
            file.ContentType,
            file.Size,
            ProofAssetRules.IsAllowedLength(file.Size)
                ? file.OpenReadStream(ProofAssetRules.MaximumAssetBytes)
                : Stream.Null);

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the Add tile is still drawn: the design withdraws it once five photographs have been
    /// chosen.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A method rather than the comparison written in the <c>@@if</c>, for two reasons. The first is
    /// NFR-14's source scan: a <c>&lt;</c> inside a markup condition opens a tag as far as that scan
    /// is concerned, and everything before it is then read as rendered English. The second is that
    /// static rendering dispatches no events, so nothing in the suite can choose a file — lifted
    /// here, the rule is asserted directly for every count including the ones only a caller going
    /// round this control can produce.
    /// <para>
    /// Withdrawing the tile is the whole of the rule. Nothing here refuses a file: the panel is not
    /// a second, stricter definition of a valid proof (AD-9), so a sixth photograph picked another
    /// way still reaches <c>CaptureProofCommandValidator</c> and is refused there by the rule that
    /// names the limit — which is the same answer the REST surface gives.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="chosen">How many files the driver has picked.</param>
    internal static bool OffersAnotherPhoto(int chosen) => chosen < ProofAssetRules.MaximumPhotos;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the selection is larger than a proof may carry, which the count says in words rather
    /// than as a figure.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Reachable because picking adds rather than replaces: four and then three is seven. A badge
    /// reading "7 / 5" states a count above the ceiling in the shape it uses for a valid one, which
    /// reads as a quota that has simply grown - so the badge says the selection is over the limit
    /// instead, and the sentence beneath it still names the ceiling.
    /// <para>
    /// Not a refusal. The files are still sent and <c>CaptureProofCommandValidator</c> is still what
    /// turns them down (AD-9); this only decides what the pill says.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="chosen">How many files the driver has picked.</param>
    internal static bool IsOverTheLimit(int chosen) => chosen > ProofAssetRules.MaximumPhotos;

    /// <summary>
    /// The dropoff as the summary card shows it: its resolved address, or its coordinates when none
    /// has resolved (FR-94).
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <see cref="LocationText"/>'s reading, shared with both delivery screens. A second copy of the
    /// fallback here would be a driver reading one thing on the row they pressed and another on the
    /// panel that opened - about the same door.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="location">The stored point.</param>
    private static string Describe(LocationView location) => LocationText.Describe(location);

    /// <summary>The figures of the delivery window, as the own-deliveries row states them (FR-100).</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <see cref="CellText"/>'s reading, for the reason <see cref="Describe"/> is shared: the window
    /// is the promise this capture is closing, and the panel must not word it differently from the
    /// row above it.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="delivery">The row this capture is filed against.</param>
    private static string Window(AssignedDeliverySummary delivery) =>
        CellText.Window(delivery.WindowEarliestAt, delivery.WindowLatestAt);
}
